--TEST--
Core\Task::afterResponse throws a catchable `RuntimeError` when deferred work calls it, and work the request defers runs in the order it was added
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Deferred work may not defer more work. The call throws an error,
// and the deferred work can catch it and carry on.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "deferred ran\n";
    try {
        Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
            echo "this never runs\n";
        });
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        echo "refused: ", $e->message, "\n";
    }
});
echo "response written\n";

// To run two pieces of work after the response, add both from the request.
// They run in the order they were added.
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "first\n";
});
Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    echo "second\n";
});
--EXPECT--
response written
deferred ran
refused: Core\Task::afterResponse: only the request's own task may defer work, and this is a child task — a `Core\Task` child or deferred work itself; hand it back to the request that started you
first
second
